v.rationale:
I ranked impl-1 first (8.5) and impl-2 second (6.5). Both pass their suites (impl-1: 36 tests; impl-2: 32 tests). Both store only a SHA-256 hash of the token, use a race-safe conditional update for single use, rate-limit on lowercased email + IP, and validate return_to with nearly the same regex.

The ranking comes down to two security bugs in impl-2, both confirmed with probes:
1. A failed accept flashes the plaintext token into the database-backed session, so the token becomes readable from our database, which the task forbids. impl-1 prevents this with `$exceptions->dontFlash(['token'])` and has a test for it.
2. impl-2 authorizes in the controller, after the FormRequest has validated. A non-owner sending an unregistered or invalid email gets 422 instead of 403, which also lets any authenticated user check whether an email is registered. impl-1 authorizes with `can:` route middleware first and tests exactly this case.

impl-1 also matches emails consistently regardless of case and whitespace, and builds the link from config('app.url'). Its weaknesses are small:
- mail is sent outside a transaction, so a send failure leaves an orphan invitation;
- a `$dontFlash` property on the FormRequest that does nothing;
- a raw `DB::table` insert instead of the relationship;
- slightly more machinery: a once() lookup in after() and a user_id column.

impl-2 does have the tidier Action classes and the transactional mail send. Those do not outweigh the token leak and the 403 bug.
